    "narrative": "The heart of our lineup today features two standout anchors: **Corbin Carroll** (73.39) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92). **Corbin Carroll** remains a priority start despite a marginal -4% penalty for facing a pitcher with an elite hold; his intrinsic ability to swipe bags makes him a cornerstone even against Clay Holmes. Meanwhile, **Vinnie Pasquantino** draws the most favorable matchup of the day against Max Scherzer (5.31 xERA). The significant 19% SP skill bonus and 14% platoon advantage push him into elite territory, making him our primary candidate for run production. **Seiya Suzuki** (65.61) also offers excellent value, benefiting from a favorable 86% stolen base opportunity window against Brandon Pfaadt\u2019s slow delivery, which overrides his minor platoon friction.\n\nOur most difficult bench decision involved **Austin Riley** (42.28), who fell just shy of the active roster threshold. While his raw base projection remains strong, the combination of a daunting matchup against Tyler Glasnow\u2014which triggered a 19% reduction in SP skill\u2014and a bottom-of-the-order projection made him a liability compared to the higher floor provided by **Josh Naylor** (57.04). We are also navigating a high-risk environment with **Mickey Moniak** (54.67), whose value is severely dampened by a 15% platoon penalty against Andrew Alvarez. Given that **James Wood** remains on the IL, we lack the depth to pivot away from these marginal plays, forcing us to lean heavily on the speed-oriented upside of **Jose Caballero** (58.58), who provides a late-order spark despite the platoon risk against Ethan Pecko. \n\nManagers should keep a close eye on the official lineups, as every active player\u2014including **Gabriel Moreno**, **Brice Turang**, **Max Muncy**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Isaac Paredes**, and **Zach McKinstry**\u2014is currently operating under \"Lineup Pending\" status. While the projections for **Gabriel Moreno** and **Isaac Paredes** are bolstered by projected #3 spots, any drop in the batting order could immediately shift their efficiency scores below our 60-point threshold for \"Strong\" starts. We remain committed to maximizing stolen base opportunities via **Corbin Carroll** and **Zach McKinstry**, but we are tempering expectations for **Max Muncy** (55.13), whose lower-order placement and negative platoon dynamics make him a candidate for early substitution if he fails to capitalize on the park factors.",
